Hermes Change Log
=================

Version 2.0.0 -- TBD
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Hermes now aims to behave exactly like GWT's own i18n code generation, so some
interfaces and properties files that worked in 1.2 behave differently or are
rejected. Review the breaking changes before upgrading.

Breaking Changes:
* Java 8 or later and ICU4J 74.2 (was ICU 4.6) are required.
* The proxy and plural classes moved out of com.mattbertolini.hermes:
  - MessagesProxy, GwtPlural, CustomPlural and Plural are now in
    com.mattbertolini.hermes.messages
  - ConstantsProxy and ConstantsWithLookupProxy are now in
    com.mattbertolini.hermes.constants
* The proxy internals were rewritten. AbstractI18nProxy and the proxy
  constructors now take a ResourceList instead of a Properties object, and
  Hermes is a final utility class that cannot be instantiated.
* Hermes.get(Class, String) no longer accepts a null locale and throws
  IllegalArgumentException. Use the new Hermes.get(Class), "default" or an
  empty string for GWT's default locale.
* MethodHandler.handle receives an empty array, never null, for methods
  without parameters.
* Number/date formatting and plural rules always use the requested locale.
  Previously they used the locale of the properties file that was found, or the
  JVM default locale when falling back to the default file.
* Locale IDs are canonicalized (en_us is treated as en_US), and private-use
  variants use GWT's file names (Foo_en_US_X-PRIV instead of Foo_en_US_X_PRIV).
* A message's alternate forms come only from the file or annotations that
  define its base message, so @AlternateMessage forms no longer leak into a
  translated locale whose file defines only the base message.
* With several @PluralCount/@Select parameters, each selector commits to its
  best form in turn (exact value, then plural form or value, then "other"), as
  GWT's generated code does.
* Map constants are unmodifiable, keep declaration order, and throw
  MissingResourceException for a listed key with no value (previously null).
* Interfaces GWT refuses to compile now throw IllegalArgumentException:
  - Constants methods returning a type other than String, int, float, double,
    boolean, String[] or Map (previously the raw string was returned)
  - float or double @Select parameters (previously a Hermes extension), boxed
    @PluralCount parameters, and parameters with both @PluralCount and @Select
  - SafeHtml arguments on methods that don't return SafeHtml
  - more than one @Default*Value annotation on a Constants method
  - an argument index beyond the method's parameters, an unterminated single
    quote, or {#} in a message without a @PluralCount parameter
Features:
* Hermes.get(Class) returns the proxy for GWT's default locale.
* JSpecify nullness annotations: every package is @NullMarked, and the
  parameters, fields and return values that may be null are marked
  @Nullable, so tools such as NullAway, the Checker Framework, IntelliJ IDEA
  and Kotlin know which values can be null.
* Locale fallback: properties files are searched in GWT's order, from the
  requested locale up to the default file, including script, region and alias
  fallbacks (zh_TW uses zh_Hant, es_MX uses es_419, iw and he are aliases).
* Properties inheritance between i18n interfaces, including multiple
  inheritance (thanks to donal-s, pull request #5). Within a locale a child
  interface takes precedence over its parents, a child's annotations take
  precedence over a parent's default file, and @DefaultLocale is honored.
* An interface needs no properties file of its own when all of its keys
  resolve elsewhere: in its annotations or in another interface's file, such
  as a child's.
* Default interface methods are supported. On Java 16 and later, interfaces
  with default methods must be public.
* Plural exact values (=n), @Offset, List and array @PluralCount parameters,
  and the inner-plural {#} argument.
* GWT's {0,list}, {0,localdatetime,skeleton} and
  {0,localdatetime,predef:NAME} message formats.
* @GenerateKeys with GWT's MD5, method name and fully qualified method name
  key generators.
* String[] and Map constants are parsed like GWT: escaped commas, trimmed
  items, and map[key]=value entries.
* Localized custom PluralRule classes are found along the full locale search
  list (Rule_pt_PT before Rule_pt).
* Nested interfaces may name their properties files Outer$Inner or
  Outer_Inner.
* Proxies implement equals and hashCode (identity) as well as toString.
* ConstantsWithLookup lookups check the constant's return type and reject
  default and static methods.
* Parsed messages and constant values are cached per method for faster
  repeated calls.
Bug Fixes:
* The proxy cache is thread-safe, and is keyed by class rather than class
  name, so same-named interfaces from different class loaders no longer
  collide or keep old class loaders alive.
* Properties file streams are closed correctly after loading.
Other:
* Built with Maven (./mvnw) instead of Ant and Ivy, with code coverage and a
  license header check.
* Depends on org.jspecify:jspecify 1.0.1, an annotations-only library with
  no runtime behavior.

Version 1.2.0 -- 2011-05-12
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Features:
* Support for GWT 2.2 and above. GWT 2.2 introduced multiple plural arguments, 
the @Select annotation, and the @AlternateMessage annotation. Support for these 
features has been added. Version 1.2 of Hermes no longer works with GWT 2.1. If 
you are using GWT 2.1, use the 1.1.x line of Hermes.

Version 1.1.0 -- 2011-04-24
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Features:
* Support for custom plural forms via the GWT PluralRule interface. This 
  follows the specification and documentation found in the GWT plural forms 
  documentation.
Bug Fixes:
* Fixed bug in unit test MessagesProxyTest where date related tests would fail 
  if run outside the America/New_York time zone (Issue #1).

Version 1.0.0 -- 2011-04-02
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Features:
* Initial release of Hermes
* Supports minimum GWT version 2.1
* Support for Messages, Constants, and ConstantsWithLookup interfaces
* UTF-8 properties file support
* Basic plural form support
* SafeHtml support
